============================================================
H. CREATE THE DOCKERFILE
============================================================
Open Dockerfile (no file extension) and write:

------------------------------------------------------------
Dockerfile
------------------------------------------------------------
FROM python:3.11-slim

WORKDIR /app

COPY requirements.txt .

RUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt

COPY app.py .

EXPOSE 5000

CMD ["python", "app.py"]
------------------------------------------------------------

Explanation of each instruction:
- FROM: Uses a Python 3.11 base image.
- WORKDIR: Sets /app as the working directory inside the image.
- COPY requirements.txt .: Copies the dependency list into the image.
- RUN: Installs the required Python packages.
- COPY app.py .: Copies the application code into the image.
- EXPOSE 5000: Documents the port used by the application.
- CMD: Starts the application when the container runs.

============================================================
I. BUILD THE DOCKER IMAGE
============================================================
STEP 1:
Open the VS Code terminal:
Terminal -> New Terminal

Make sure the terminal is inside the docker-ai-model folder.

STEP 2:
Run:

docker build -t ai-model-app .

Explanation:
- docker build: Builds an image.
- -t ai-model-app: Names the image ai-model-app.
- . : Uses the current folder as the build context.

Wait for the build to complete. The first build may take time because Docker downloads the Python image and packages.

STEP 3:
Check the image:

docker images

You should see an image named ai-model-app.

============================================================
J. RUN THE DOCKER CONTAINER
============================================================
Run:

docker run -d --name ai-model-container -p 5000:5000 ai-model-app

Explanation:
- docker run: Creates and starts a container.
- -d: Runs it in the background.
- --name ai-model-container: Assigns a name to the container.
- -p 5000:5000: Maps port 5000 on your computer to port 5000 in the container.
- ai-model-app: The image used to create the container.

============================================================
K. TEST THE APPLICATION
============================================================
STEP 1: Check the container:

docker ps

The container named ai-model-container should appear as running.

STEP 2: Test the home page
Open a browser and visit:

http://localhost:5000

Expected output:
AI Model API is running!

STEP 3: Test the prediction endpoint
The /predict endpoint accepts a POST request with JSON data.

If you have curl installed, run this in PowerShell or Terminal:

curl.exe -X POST http://localhost:5000/predict -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d "{\"area\":1200}"

Expected response will be similar to:

{"area":1200.0,"predicted_price_lakhs":60.0}

The prediction may be displayed with a slightly different decimal due to the model calculation.

Alternative testing using Python:
Create a separate file named test_api.py with:

import requests

response = requests.post(
    "http://localhost:5000/predict",
    json={"area": 1200}
)

print(response.status_code)
print(response.json())

Install requests if needed:

pip install requests

Then run:

python test_api.py

Expected:
- Status code: 200
- JSON output containing the area and predicted price.

